# Mushroom Classification

An educational classification project that compares Decision Tree and Random Forest models on the UCI Mushroom dataset. It uses mushroom characteristics to classify records as edible (`e`) or poisonous (`p`) and reports poisonous-class sensitivity alongside other test metrics.

> **Safety:** This is a machine-learning exercise, not a mushroom identification or food-safety tool. Never use its predictions to decide whether a mushroom is safe to eat.

## Features

- Downloads the UCI Mushroom dataset for training.
- One-hot encodes categorical features and imputes missing values.
- Compares Decision Tree and Random Forest classifiers.
- Calibrates a threshold on validation data to target full poisonous-class sensitivity, then reports performance on a separate test set.
- Supports predictions from a CSV containing the dataset's 22 feature columns.

## Project structure

```text
mushroom-classification/
├── requirements.txt
├── src/
│   ├── train_model.py
│   └── predict.py
└── outputs/                 # created by training
```

## Setup and run

```bash
py -m venv .venv
# Windows PowerShell:
.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1

pip install -r requirements.txt
python src/train_model.py
![output](image.png)
![output2](image-1.png)
```

Classify a CSV of mushroom features:

```bash
python src/predict.py --input path/to/mushroom_features.csv
![output](image-2.png)
```

Training saves `outputs/mushroom_models.joblib`. The dataset groups mushrooms of unknown edibility with poisonous examples. A validation sensitivity target does not guarantee the same result on unseen data or real mushrooms.

## Dataset and references

- [UCI Mushroom dataset](https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/73/mushroom)
- [scikit-learn multiclass classification](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/multiclass.html)
